The PPA workflow prepares locked source on a GitHub-hosted runner, pushes
it to main in Launchpad's lsm repository, and requests recipe builds.
Launchpad's main is generated; GitHub's main remains the source of truth.
No compilation or GPG signing happens in GitHub Actions.

Configure https://code.launchpad.net/~nmccallum/+recipe/lsm with:

    # git-build-recipe format 0.4 deb-version {debversion}+{time}
    lp:~nmccallum/pichi-vm/+git/lsm main

Select Resolute (26.04), Stonking (26.10), and Tenacious (27.04).
Disable daily builds and target ppa:nmccallum/pichi with amd64 and arm64
enabled. One prepared source serves all releases and both architectures.
Every main push publishes source and requests builds. CI finishes when
Launchpad accepts the request; building and publication continue there.

The repository Actions secret LAUNCHPAD_AUTHORIZATION contains the pichi-vm
OAuth Authorization header (consumer key, access token, PLAINTEXT signature
method, and encoded signature), authorized to change non-private data.
CI adds a timestamp and nonce, obtains a
short-lived repository Git token, and masks it before use. No GPG key or
separate Git credential is needed. Revoke access through Launchpad's
application authorizations. Authentication setup is documented at:
https://ubuntu.com/docs/launchpad/user/how-to/launchpad-web-signing/

Build prepared source offline with dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc.

The package is named pichi-lsm because Ubuntu's lsm package is an
unrelated connectivity monitor. It installs /usr/libexec/pichi-lsm,
including the BPF policy embedded by the existing build.rs. Its
pichi-lsm.service owns policy startup. The appliance configures dependencies
and ordering for its consumers. Installation does not enable or start the
service.

Source preparation includes pinned bpf-linker 0.13.0 source and vendors its
dependencies plus Rust 1.93/1.97 library dependencies, as in Arma. The build
compiles the linker with distro LLVM 22, then builds the helper and policy
offline with distro rust-src. No compiler or linker binary is bundled.
The downloaded linker manifest has its own workspace to isolate it from
LSM's workspace. Nested Cargo builds use the generated .cargo/config.toml.
